hat the threshold can occur no later than the\np value above. A more general picture is suggested by further\nexperiments.\nConjecture III.1. Let N = m + kd points be given in Sd−1,\nwith 1 ≤m < d, d ≥2, and gram matrix A ∈RN×N.\nThen there is a value of p0, independent of dimension d\nand excess m, such that the repeated orthonormal sequence\n{ej\nmod d}N\nj=1 minimizes Ep over all size N systems of unit\nvectors (with value Ep(A) = d(k2 −k) + 2k) for p < p0 and\nthe minimum value of Ep(A) satisﬁes Ep(A) < d(k2−k)+2k\nwhen p > p0. Further p0 = p0(k) satisﬁes p0(k) →2 as\nk →∞.\nIV. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\nThe author was supported in part by grant from the US Na-\ntional Science Foundation, DMS-1600693. The author would\nlike to acknowledge fruitful discussions with Alexey Glazyrin.\nThe author would also like to acknowledge helpful discussions\nabout related topics with Dmitriy Bilyk, Ryan Matzke, and\nOleksandr Vlasiuk in connection with a forthcoming paper.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Spectral Erdős–Faudree–Rousseau Theorem - Li - 2025 - Journal of Graph Theory - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/jgt.23280)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; In this paper, we have bounded the minimum number of triangular edges of a graph in terms of the spectral radius, and we have established a spectral Erdős–Faudree–Rousseau theorem. ... We stated in Section 4.3 that Theorem 4.5 can deduce a conjecture of Erdős involving the booksize of a graph.\nSuch edges are called triangular edges. In this paper, we present a spectral version of the result of Erdős, Faudree, and Rousseau. Using the supersaturation-stability and the spectral technique, we prove that every n-vertex graph G with \\lambda \\left(G\\right)\\ge \\sqrt{\\lfloor {n}^{2}∕4\\rfloor } contains at least 2\\lfloor n∕2\\rfloor -1 triangular edges, unless G is a balanced complete bipartite graph. The method in our paper has some interesting applications. Firstly, the supersaturation-stability can be used to revisit a conjecture of Erdős concerning the booksize of a graph, which was initially proved by Edwards (unpublished), and independently by Khadžiivanov and Nikiforov (1979). Secondly, our method can improve the bound on the order n of the spectral extremal graph when we forbid the friendship graph as a substructure. We drop the condition that requires the order n to be sufficiently large, which was investigated by Cioabă et al. (2020) using the triangle removal lemma. Thirdly, this method can be utilized to deduce the classical stability for odd cycles, and it gives more concise bounds on parameters. Then G is isomorphic to a subgraph of G\\left(a,b,c\\right) for some a,b,c.\n\nParticularly, Füredi and Maleki [18] proposed a numerical conjecture, which states that every n-vertex graph with m edges has at least g\\left(n,m\\right) triangular edges, where\n\ng\\left(n,m\\right)=\\mathrm{min}\\left\\{m-bc:a+b+c=n,\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{a}{2}\\right)+b\\left(a+c\\right)\\ge m\\right\\}.\n\nWe remark that Conjecture 1.2 characterizes the structures of the minimizers, while the latter conjecture gives a lower bound only. By using a generalization of Zykov's symmetrization method, Füredi and Maleki [18] showed a lower bound: if G is a graph on n vertices with m>{n}^{2}/4 edges, then G has at least g\\left(n,m\\right)-3n/2 triangular edges. Soon after, Gruslys and Letzter [19] proved an exact version of the result of Füredi and Maleki. Let \\mathbf{NT}\\left(G\\right) be the set of non-triangular edges of G. The following result was established in Ref. [19].\n\nTheorem 1.3. ((Gruslys–Letzter, 2018))There is {n}_{0} such that for any graph G on n\\ge {n}_{0} vertices, there exists a graph H=G\\left(a,b,c\\right) on n vertices such that e\\left(H\\right)\\ge e\\left(G\\right) and \\mid \\mathbf{NT}\\left(H\\right)\\mid \\ge \\mid \\mathbf{NT}\\left(G\\right)\\mid .\n\nTheorem 1.3 shows that for sufficiently large n, the minimum number of triangular edges among all n-vertex graphs with at least m edges is achieved by the graph G\\left(a,b,c\\right) or its subgraph for some a,b,c. We refer the readers to Ref. [19] for more details and Ref. [20] for the study on the minimum number of edges that occur in odd cycles.\n\n### 4.3 Applications of the Supersaturation-Stability\n\nThere are several advantages to the supersaturation-stability method. As promised, we now present four quick applications of this method. In our framework, we will take advantage of the results in Theorem 4.5 or Corollary 4.6 with some appropriate structural analysis.\n\n#### 4.3.1 The Erdős Conjecture Involving the Booksize\n\nRecall that a book of size t consists of t triangles that share a common edge. The study of bounding the largest size of a book in a graph was initially investigated by Erdős [4], who proved that every n-vertex graph with at least \\lfloor {n}^{2}/4\\rfloor +1 edges contains a book of size n/6-O\\left(1\\right), and conjectured that the term O\\left(1\\right) can be removed. This conjecture was later proved by Edwards (unpublished, see [17, Lemma 4]) and independently by Khadžiivanov and Nikiforov [64] (unavailable, see [65]). Unfortunately, neither of the two original references can be found. Here, we show that Theorem 4.5 can easily confirm the Erdős conjecture. More precisely, we can use Theorem 4.5 to prove that every graph G on n vertices with more than {n}^{2}/4 edges contains a book of size greater than n/6. Indeed, assume that G has exactly t triangles, then Theorem 4.5 yields that G is not 6t/n-far from being bipartite. Specifically, one can remove less than 6t/n edges from G to destroy all t triangles. So one of these edges must be contained in more than n/6 triangles, as needed. For more related results, we refer the readers to [40, 41, 65] and the references therein.\n\n#### 4.3.2 Eliminating the Use of Triangle Removal Lemma\n\nIn 2020, Cioabă et al. [49] studied the spectral extremal graphs of order n for the friendship graph {F}_{k} and sufficiently large n, where {F}_{k} is the graph that consists of k triangles sharing a vertex. Their proof uses the Ruzsa–Szemerédi triangle removal lemma, which settles the problem in the case where k is fixed, and the result is meaningless when k is large and grows with n (say, when k\\ge \\mathrm{log}n). ## 7 Concluding Remarks\n\nIn this paper, we have bounded the minimum number of triangular edges of a graph in terms of the spectral radius, and we have established a spectral Erdős–Faudree–Rousseau theorem. The main ideas in our proof attribute to the supersaturation-stability (Theorem 4.5) and some additional spectral techniques. We believe that this method may have the potential to be applied to a wider range of spectral extremal graph problems.\n\n### 7.1 Supersaturation-Stability via Spectral Radius\n\nWe stated in Section 4.3 that Theorem 4.5 can deduce a conjecture of Erdős involving the booksize of a graph. It is worth mentioning that an interesting spectral problem of Zhai and Lin [40, Problem 1.2] asserts that every n-vertex graph G with \\lambda \\left(G\\right)>\\lambda \\left({T}_{n,2}\\right) has booksize greater than n/6 as well. To solve this problem, it is sufficient to show a spectral version of Theorem 4.5. For the sake of formality, we propose the following conjecture.\nThis leads to an improvement on the coefficients of Lemma 4.7 under the constraint n\\ge 4{k}^{3}. However, it seems difficult to improve the exponent of k. In the case of Turán number, Erdős et al. [74] proved that for k\\ge 1 and n\\ge 50{k}^{2}, we have\n\n\\text{ex}\\left(n,{F}_{k}\\right)=⌊\\frac{{n}^{2}}{4}⌋+\\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll}{k}^{2}-k,\\hfill & \\phantom{\\rule{0.1em}{0ex}}\\text{if}\\phantom{\\rule{0.1em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}k\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.1em}{0ex}}\\text{is odd};\\hfill \\\\ {k}^{2}-{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}}k,\\hfill & \\phantom{\\rule{0.1em}{0ex}}\\text{if}\\phantom{\\rule{0.1em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}k\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.1em}{0ex}}\\text{is even}.\\hfill \\end{array}\\right.\n\nFurthermore, they conjectured [74, p. 90] that the above result on \\text{ex}\\left(n,{F}_{k}\\right) still holds for every n\\ge 4k, rather than n\\ge 50{k}^{2}. This conjecture remains unresolved. In the case of spectral radius, we may ask further whether the spectral extremal result for {F}_{k} still holds for every n\\ge Ck with an absolute constant C>0. We mention that finding (linear) sharp bounds on the order of graphs is also regarded as an interesting problem in extremal graph theory; we refer the readers to Refs. [32, 40, 75] for some related results.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRepeated Minimizers of $p$-Frame Energies (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/347824093_Repeated_Minimizers_of_p-Frame_Energies)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Repeated Minimizers of p-Frame Energies ... In this paper, we connect the problem of minimizing this value to another optimization problem, thus giving new lower bounds for such energies. ... We also prove that for $1\\leq m<d$, a repeated orthonormal basis construction of N=d+m vectors minimizes the energy over an interval, $p\\in[1,p_m]$, and demonstrates an analogous result for all N in the case d=2. ... In this section, we use an induction argument to deal with the case of odd N , and provide a proof of Theorem 1.1.\n\n# Repeated Minimizers of p-Frame Energies\n\n  * November 2020\n  * SIAM Journal on Discrete Mathematics 34(4):2411-2423\n\nDOI:10.1137/19M1282702\n\nAuthors:\n\nAlexey Glazyrin\n\nAlexey Glazyrin\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nJosiah Park\n\nJosiah Park\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a collection of N unit vectors ${X}=\\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^N$, define the p-frame energy of ${X}$ as the quantity $\\sum_{i\\neq j} |\\langle x_i,x_j \\rangle|^p$. In this paper, we connect the problem of minimizing this value to another optimization problem, thus giving new lower bounds for such energies. In particular, for $p<2$, we prove that this energy is at least $2(N-d) p^{-\\frac p 2} (2-p)^{\\frac {p-2} 2}$ which is sharp for $d\\leq N\\leq 2d$ and p=1. We also prove that for $1\\leq m<d$, a repeated orthonormal basis construction of N=d+m vectors minimizes the energy over an interval, $p\\in[1,p_m]$, and demonstrates an analogous result for all N in the case d=2. Finally, we give conjectures on these and other energies.\n\n## Citations (8)\n\n## References (15)\n\n... One of the objectives of this paper to shed some new lights on these cases. In particular, we will extend a result proved in [13] which states that if 0 < p ≤ 1.3 the absolute minimum of FP p,N,2 for odd N is (N − 1) 2 /2, which is achieved by X ⊥ N . We show that the result still holds when p ≤ log 3 log 2 ≈ 0.58. ...\n\n... In this section, we use an induction argument to deal with the case of odd N , and provide a proof of Theorem 1.1. This induction argument is inspired by the method used in [13], which we refine and extend to a larger range of p. ...\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaximum Modulus of Independence Roots of Graphs and Trees (https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.09775)\nciteturn3academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.9 years ago; Title: Maximum Modulus of Independence Roots of Graphs and Trees ... We bound the maximum modulus, $\\mbox{maxmod}(n)$, of an independence root over all graphs on $n$ vertices and the maximum modulus, $\\mbox{maxmod}_{T}(n)$, of an independence root over all trees on $n$ vertices in terms of $n$.\nTitle: Maximum Modulus of Independence Roots of Graphs and Trees\nAuthors: Jason I. Brown, Ben Cameron\nDate: Sun Dec 23 21:17:30 2018\n\nThe independence polynomial of a graph is the generating polynomial for the number of independent sets of each size and its roots are called independence roots. We bound the maximum modulus, $\\mbox{maxmod}(n)$, of an independence root over all graphs on $n$ vertices and the maximum modulus, $\\mbox{maxmod}_{T}(n)$, of an independence root over all trees on $n$ vertices in terms of $n$. In particular, we show that $$\\frac{\\log_3(\\mbox{maxmod}(n))}{n}=\\frac{1}{3}+o(1)$$ and $$\\frac{\\log_2(\\mbox{maxmod}_{T}(n))}{n}=\\frac{1}{2}+o(1).$$--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRepeated minimizers of $p$-frame energies (https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.06096)\nciteturn3academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; Title: Repeated minimizers of $p$-frame energies ... In this paper, we connect the problem of minimizing this value to another optimization problem, so giving new lower bounds for such energies. ... We prove that for $1\\leq m<d$, a repeated orthonormal basis construction of $N=d+m$ vectors minimizes the energy over an interval, $p\\in[1,p_m]$, and demonstrate an analogous result for all $N$ in the case $d=2$.\nTitle: Repeated minimizers of $p$-frame energies\nAuthors: Alexey Glazyrin, Josiah Park\nDate: Fri Jan 18 06:15:20 2019\n\nFor a collection of $N$ unit vectors $\\mathbf{X}=\\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^N$, define the $p$-frame energy of $\\mathbf{X}$ as the quantity $\\sum_{i\\neq j} |\\langle x_i,x_j \\rangle|^p$. In this paper, we connect the problem of minimizing this value to another optimization problem, so giving new lower bounds for such energies. In particular, for $p<2$, we prove that this energy is at least $2(N-d) p^{-\\frac p 2} (2-p)^{\\frac {p-2} 2}$ which is shar